WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:01.090
(soft ambient music)

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:03.870
- [Narrator] As a global health
emergency is taking hold,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.280
America is moving forward

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.600
with its crucial once-a-decade census.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.970
In March, the Census Bureau
said an employee tested positive

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.880
for the new coronavirus.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.080
The worker didn't have
contact with the public,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.280
but the incident is
reinforcing the bureau's need

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.770
to collect information over the internet.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.460
This isn't the bureau's only challenge

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.000
as it collects surveys.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.700
The 2020 census has been on

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:28.410
the Government Accountability
Office's watch list

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:29.850
for three years.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.200
Those concerns, combined with
pandemic-driven impediments,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.500
could make 2020's count
more difficult to execute,

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:37.940
but the count must move forward

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.390
because it is a foundational
part of American governance.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:42.670
- The census is very important

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:46.420
because it is the foundation
of how political power

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.920
is distributed in this country.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.330
And so, it's important to be counted,

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.360
and it's not just that people be included

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.140
but be included where they reside

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.480
so that political power can
be equitably distributed

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.820
or accurately distributed.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.100
- [Narrator] Here's how the new dynamics

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.923
are reshaping 2020's count.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.130
This year, most households
received a letter

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.680
rather than a complete
census form in the mail.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.170
- This is the first census
in which there'll be

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.870
a widespread option to
respond over the internet.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:11.820
- [Narrator] That's because the census

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.920
wanted to move toward online response.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.440
In light of the new coronavirus,

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.430
the bureau's reemphasizing
the need to respond online

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.370
or by phone or by mail

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.430
without having to meet a census-taker.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.050
This is the safest way
to answer the census,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.030
and that could prove crucial
as the country hunkers down

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.380
to increase social distance

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.010
and limit the spread of the virus.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.590
Online response will limit the amount

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.490
of in-person enumeration
needed to count everyone,

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:38.160
which relates to another major challenge

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.480
facing 2020's census: staffing.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.690
Staffing has been an
ongoing area of concern

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:45.230
at the Census Bureau.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:48.330
That problem becomes most
visible at the local level.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.880
- One of the things that's very important

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.310
is to look at the census down
at almost a granular level,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.770
a very local level.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:56.100
That's what's gonna make
or break the census.

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.660
- [Narrator] Only about
two-thirds of the country

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.000
responded to the initial
prompt for the 2010 census,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.140
and those numbers vary at the local level.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.730
For example, about 74% of Minnesotans

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.470
responded to the census's first request,

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.870
but some counties in the state
had response rates below 40%.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.190
Lower response rates mean
that the Census Bureau

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:18.190
needs to send temporary
workers to knock on doors

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.740
and request responses in person.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.500
But the need to increase social distance

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.450
will make it harder to
send field workers out

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.420
to track hard-to-reach populations

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.623
and others who don't respond.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.060
For months, the Census Bureau struggled

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.370
to recruit enough temporary workers.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.390
The GAO issued this chart in February

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.250
to show how far behind
the bureau had fallen.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.470
The Census Bureau disputed those findings,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.600
saying that they effectively
met their staffing goals,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.130
but the bureau will
still have to deal with

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.910
falling response rates.

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.640
That's because Americans in general

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.100
have become less likely to answer surveys.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.660
- Each census that I've
been involved with,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.170
the bureau assumes a
lower mail response rate

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.040
than they received in
the preceding census,

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.650
in part because of just the
complexity of counting people

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.190
in various living arrangements,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.070
but in part because of reduction in trust.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.890
- [Narrator] Which brings us
to a third major challenge

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:09.430
facing 2020's census: trust.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:12.100
In 2019, the Republican National Committee

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.910
sent a 2020 Congressional District Census

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.660
to a number of households.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.080
It was not an official census.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.260
The document asks about
political preferences

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.000
and preferred news sources,

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.500
none of which are asked
on the official census.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.030
The questionnaire was also much longer

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.360
than the official census
and asked respondents

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.020
to donate money to process the document.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.930
As recently as March, the Trump campaign

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.550
promoted the misleading
prompt on Facebook.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.210
Facebook later removed the ads,

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:41.230
citing the company's
census interference policy.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:43.010
But experts say that the lingering effects

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.900
of confusing documents like this

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.110
could further depress
response rates in the country.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:51.390
Census forms started arriving
in mailboxes in mid-March.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.450
The bureau must report results to Congress

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.760
no later than December of 2020.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.290
Some response timetables
could be shaken up

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.340
as the bureau monitors the situation

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.536
and takes new guidance from
public health authorities.

00:04:03.536 --> 00:04:06.619
(soft ambient music)

